Practical Use Case: Managing Environmental Risk at an Open-Pit Mine
Consider an illustrative open-pit mine experiencing increased rainfall during the wet season.

Environmental inspections identify sediment accumulation near a drainage channel. Monitoring results also show a change in downstream water-quality conditions.

The environmental team needs to establish whether the observations are related, determine the cause and take appropriate action.

A connected digital EHS workflow could operate as follows:

Detection: Record the inspection finding and relevant water-quality measurements.

Assessment: Review the monitoring data, site conditions and applicable permit requirements.

Investigation: Identify potential causes, including drainage conditions and sediment-control effectiveness.

Corrective action: Assign appropriate operational and engineering measures to responsible personnel.

Verification: Complete follow-up inspections and sampling to evaluate the effectiveness of controls.

Management review: Examine recurring findings and update the environmental risk assessment where necessary.

The value of the system lies in connecting observations, responsibilities and evidence of completion.

It does not determine the technical cause automatically, nor does it replace environmental sampling or professional assessment.

